WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:27.500
New Care Models in Tertiary Mental Health Services

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.460
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Firstly, can I add that is
a privilege to work with Claire and Stephen.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:41.019
Both of them have an accomplished track record
as successful chief executives of significant

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:47.359
mental health organisations, so their expertise
in helping us move mental health forward is

00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:52.040
very, very welcome in the organisation, so
thank you for that.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.679
One of the issues that we have to address
is that mental health services for young people

00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:03.929
are afflicted by fragmentation – fragmentation
of geography, fragmentation of care – and

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:14.430
that in turn is related in some ways to fragmented
funding and accountability. This paper that

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.460
Claire and Stephen will talk about reports
a proposal from our Chief Executive to address

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:25.581
these issues through what might be described
as a modest proof of concept initiative, where

00:01:25.581 --> 00:01:33.041
secondary mental health volunteer trusts will
manage tertiary services.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.700
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: It is a real privilege to
be at my first NHS England Board meeting probably

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:46.890
some 100 days in post, there or thereabouts.
Before handing over to Stephen Firn who will

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.700
talk about the particular paper in front of
us, I am hoping to take the liberty to very

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:58.640
briefly, in a few brief moments, remind the
Board of the context within which this particular

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:05.660
programme of work is landing for mental health.
The Board will remember that NHS England commissioned

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:12.100
an independent task force to report on our
mental health, England’s mental health,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.650
and that reported in February of this year
and made 58 cross-government recommendations

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:25.099
to improve England’s mental health.
In July of this year, we in NHS England published

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:32.730
our implementation plan which, I think it
is fair to say, was full of muscle in terms

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.840
of the amount of detail it gave around who
we will be treating, when we will be treating

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:46.040
them and where the money will flow. In a nutshell,
our aspiration is to provide better access

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:52.769
to evidence-based treatment for an additional
one&nbsp;million people by investing an addition

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:58.900
£1 billion, and the action plan that we have
published, the Five Year Forward View implementation

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.760
plan, captures the detail of that.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:10.659
Our plan has various work programmes across
the life course, affecting and changing mental

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:17.480
health across the health system, in acute
settings, in primary care and in the community.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:25.360
It is also a plan designed to deliver better
outcomes across diverse populations, and it

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.930
is a plan designed to improve the integration
of physical and mental health, treating the

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:36.909
whole person; let us stop some of these silos.
We have then been working with the STPs on

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:43.939
ensuring that our plan is manifest in their
proposals, and we were delighted when, on

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:52.799
22nd September, NHS England was able to secure
really considerable space in the planning

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:58.389
guidance for the implementation of the plan.
These are all things that we have put in place

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:03.529
to absolutely help the system drive the delivery
of an ambitious agenda.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:13.980
Just very quickly, I would like to give the
Board a flavour of some of the work, if I

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:20.230
might, that is ongoing already around different
programmes, very, very briefly. For example,

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:28.690
at the moment we have just issued a call for
bids to providers to extend our talking therapies.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:35.940
Last year, we saw 900,000 people in England
for talking therapies, and we have a plan

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:42.820
to see 600,000 more, in particular focusing
on those living with chronic and complex physical

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:48.570
health problems who would benefit from psychological
therapies. It is also part of our plan to

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:55.370
deliver 3,000 more therapists in primary care.
We will be announcing very, very soon where

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.680
our first-wave pilots of this next push will
be.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:07.810
Another example of work that is ongoing at
the moment is that we are about to identify

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:13.410
and then announce pilot sites for delivering
new models of perinatal care. This will be

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:18.190
an announcement we will be making shortly.
We gave a commitment to see 30,000 more women

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:27.560
by 2021 who are suffering from perinatal conditions,
so that will be happening really soon. We

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:36.300
are just finalising plans to identify where
we will be setting up four new mother-and-baby

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:41.580
units across the country, linked to our community
push.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:48.910
Last week, we announced the release of £25
million to assist with the waiting time targets,

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:54.340
capability and capacity, in child and adolescent
mental health services. We have seen an additional

00:05:54.340 --> 00:06:00.590
£149 million going into the baselines this
year of CCGs, to treat more children, remembering

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:07.210
that our aim is to treat 70,000 more children
and young people by 2021. We have released

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.040
an additional £25,000 to help with some of
the waiting lists and pressures of the here

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:17.290
and now, and next year we are releasing a
further £170 million to help with those plans.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:22.870
I am nearly at the end of where this piece
fits in, but just another example or two,

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:28.690
if I might. We will in the short term be opening
some more CAMHS tier 4 beds. We know that

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:33.110
there is a pressure in the system now, but
it is not national, it is in certain areas.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:40.720
That work is well underway, and in October
we will know where we need to place additional

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.700
capacity into the system.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:48.650
Finally, for example, we have recently put
an additional £5 million into the urgent

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:53.930
and emergency care vanguards, to look at crisis
and emergency treatment for children and young

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:57.920
people.
We are doing so much more than that; read

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.630
the implementation plan and the planning guidance.
However, I just wanted to give the Board a

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:07.710
flavour of the tremendous work programme that
is underway around the Five Year Forward View

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.690
for mental health.
At that point, I would like to hand over to

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:16.090
Stephen, who has been working with colleagues
in specialist commissioning and the mental

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:21.570
health team, around a very exciting programme
of work in terms of new models of care.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.060
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: For the last five months,
I have been leading this programme. Prior

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:34.310
to that, I was chief executive of a mental
health trust for 14 years, and I recall that

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:41.170
on numerous occasions we had to place acutely
disturbed young people in private placement,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:46.400
out of area, many miles from home. However,
local clinicians told me this could have been

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:53.120
avoided if we had had in place local crisis
and home treatment services for young people,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.680
which exist in some parts of the country but
not all. That is an example of the type of

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.960
service gap this programme is hoping to address.
If I could give you a specific example in

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:08.980
the paper: the Tees, Esk &amp; Wear Valleys FT.
They are one of the proposed sites; they calculate

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:13.980
that within the first 12 months, they could
reduce occupied bed days out of area by about

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.740
3,500, which would generate savings which
would be more than enough to develop a local

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:25.740
crisis and home treatment team for young children.
What we are doing, in essence, is taking money

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:32.250
that we are already spending and reinvesting
savings from that in local services to provide

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:37.460
better care. I hope that gives a sense of
what this programme is about and why we established

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.460
it.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:43.950
Just a few headlines on where we have got
to so far. A call for applications went to

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:51.550
every provider of tertiary mental health services
in the country, including public sector, NHS,

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:58.770
private sector and the charitable sector.
There as a fantastic response: 36 sites, partnerships,

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:03.870
applied to be part of the programme, which
represented over 100 providers. We have selected

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:09.490
six&nbsp;sites which, again, include the public,
charitable and private sector, and you will

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:15.420
see those six sites in your paper. Each of
those have agreed to establish shadow arrangements

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:22.190
from 1st October, which will enable us to
work with them to develop full business cases

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:29.380
by 23rd December, which will robustly lay
out over the next two years their plans to

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:35.640
reduce occupied bed days, develop local services,
generate savings and reinvest in things for

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:41.260
young people and adults. Subject to appropriate
due diligence and agreeing risk shares with

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:48.050
those business cases, we plan to transfer
care budgets to those providers from 1st April

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:54.640
2017, to give them the levers and the incentives
to deliver this better care closer to home.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.280
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I think this is to be
welcomed. I am hardly going to sit around

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:18.320
here and slag it off, but I think the out-of-area
stuff has been a wicked problem for a long

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:25.340
time. I am curious about your ambition for
it, because I think we have to go for this.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:33.930
We spend a lot of money on this, and it has
actually driven poor market management. I

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.850
am interested in what your ambition is for
this.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:43.339
Claire, you and I sat on the review of the
police response to mental health in London,

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:48.920
and we looked at some pretty shocking cases
of not very good crisis management, which

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:53.990
was not necessarily about the police but about
the NHS. What I am curious about is, as a

00:10:53.990 --> 00:11:06.460
result of where we have got to in mental health,
what can an individual in crisis expect to

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:14.120
receive that is better as a result of where
we have got to in the delivery of the new

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:20.920
mental healthcare plans? What can someone
in crisis expect that is different?

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:24.040
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Are you talking about in
the context of the broader plan, or what will

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.490
our new care models bring?
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: No, in the broader plan.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.020
So my question to Stephen is basically: what
is your ambition for the out-of-area placements?

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:33.550
Where can we go with this? My question to
Claire is: what can an individual in crisis

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:37.410
expect? Because that is at the point of most
risk.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:44.290
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: The ambition is that, if we
do approve these business cases, we will be

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:54.410
transferring around £320 million of expenditure
to those six sites. One of the principles

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:00.250
is that they will take 100% risk on that expenditure;
they all recognise that and will build that

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:06.990
into their business plans. At this stage they
are all confident that they could make significant

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:14.650
savings, stressing that the savings come from
the better care closer to home. Obviously,

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:19.529
we need to work through the detail in the
business plans, but in all six of those sites

00:12:19.529 --> 00:12:25.460
I would expect to see robust new community
services delivering care to people who were

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:31.920
previously placed out of area, and I would
be very disappointed if their cumulative downside

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:38.630
cases did not add up to the tens of millions,
from that £320 million budget. I will obviously

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:46.890
be able to confirm that more come December.
There is then the consideration about whether

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:52.210
we want to roll this out further into a second
wave. There are certainly lots of people queuing

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:56.690
up who are interested, but we have not made
a final decision about that at this stage.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:04.260
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Why would we not, actually?
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: I tried to give the Board

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:09.340
just a snapshot of some of the work we are
doing, and actually I would love to make the

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:14.640
offer of a seminar or something to help the
Board see the whole programme and where we

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.290
are, if, Sir Malcolm, you thought at the right
time that would be helpful? It is something

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:23.460
we would be delighted to do.
Briefly, some things I did not mention. The

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.850
plan is really clear about our ambition to
reduce out-of-area treatments for people in

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:34.460
crisis across the age range. In order to do
that, you need a whole system to be working

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:39.060
better. So the first thing I would say in
answer to your question about crisis is it

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:45.310
is really important we are working cross-government
with housing, education and local authorities,

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:52.710
because we know– and you know as well as
I – the things that can precipitate crisis

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:58.480
are often gaps in social care or problems
with broader life. The first thing I would

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:04.399
say is, our NHS England priorities sit firmly
within that broader cross-government push

00:14:04.399 --> 00:14:08.640
to better mental health.
The kinds of things that we more specifically

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:14.440
are focusing on in the plan, that we anticipate
will help individuals who do find themselves

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:21.500
in crisis: the plan is very clear about better
access to liaison services. We are saying

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:28.250
by 2021, we will have crisis liaison services
in all major hospitals across the country,

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:34.960
for example. We are investing more and developing
the capability and capacity of home treatment

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:41.779
teams, so that we can deliver more crisis
care to adults at home or closer to home,

00:14:41.779 --> 00:14:48.720
as well. We are doing considerable work on
places of safety and Section 136, which was

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.590
one of the major focuses of our work in London
all that while ago. The plan is actually very

00:14:55.590 --> 00:15:01.899
focused on adults, the whole pathway; how
to prevent crisis and enable people to live

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:06.160
well but, when they are in crisis, to make
sure that we have the beds, we are not sending

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:10.860
them out of area, we are making that pathway
work through better liaison and better home

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:16.209
treatment for people in crisis. Of course,
one knows it is not just partnership work

00:15:16.209 --> 00:15:22.710
that helps avoid crisis; it is better working
with individuals around their plans and relapse

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:27.230
prevention, so that is all in our implementation
plan.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:36.910
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Likewise, I view this paper
very positively, it is very welcome. I just

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:45.220
wanted to ask: Claire referred to the fact
that current problems are not evenly spread;

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:51.490
some places are worse than others, some places
are doing better than others. How does this

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:59.230
selection of areas map onto the problem areas,
as it were? Because willing people are not

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:05.790
necessarily the places that are most in need.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: No. I would very much like

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:11.550
Stephen to answer that. Partly why I wanted
to set the context of the broader work we

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:16.649
are doing around the Five Year Forward View
is that we are looking nationally about the

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:21.649
work programmes we are taking forward, and
we are identifying areas of greatest need,

00:16:21.649 --> 00:16:26.870
and we will be targeting additional support
and focus to quality improvement in those

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:33.001
areas. That was not the basis upon which Stephen
and his team selected these pilot sites, because

00:16:33.001 --> 00:16:38.990
I cannot emphasise enough the extent to which
this really is new models of care breaking

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:43.519
considerable new ground. Perhaps you could
say something about how you selected them

00:16:43.519 --> 00:16:46.399
and why?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: Well, the six we selected

00:16:46.399 --> 00:16:51.029
had to go through a shortlisting process and
then a panel interview, which included clinical

00:16:51.029 --> 00:16:56.930
experts, experts by experience, and people
like myself who have run mental health services.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:17:02.370
Because fundamental to this is that they have
the expertise and the clinical leadership

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:08.860
to do this safely. We will not be bringing
back people from out-of-area placements locally

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:14.680
without being sure that they are good, safe
and effective local services. That was the

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:20.780
number one criterion.
Secondly, once they were above that line we

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:25.839
were looking for different sizes. So they
range from around about £8&nbsp;million in a

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:32.559
single trust, which I just referred to, in
Tees, Esk &amp; Wear, to a £105 million budget

00:17:32.559 --> 00:17:38.059
which is the West Midlands, which is a partnership
of a range of different trusts. Essentially,

00:17:38.059 --> 00:17:44.970
with the business cases, we will be testing
which model works best. They are all lead

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:50.790
providers and all sound lead providers, so
they do not have any big regulatory issues

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:57.890
or financial issues. Inevitably some are stronger
than others, but they are all above the line.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:03.230
The question will be, if we do roll out to
a second wave, that we maintain the quality.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:09.240
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: If I might also just add:
one of the things I did not say in the introduction

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:14.630
but I think is incredibly important to the
question you asked, is the Chief Executive

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:22.559
and the team here have been working, together
with myself and others, on preparing to publish

00:18:22.559 --> 00:18:31.140
unprecedented levels of information about
access, quality, performance and spend. This

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:36.170
publication is due very, very soon, and what
it will do – for the first time ever, actually,

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:45.400
for mental health – is show us what is happening
CCG by CCG across the country in those domains.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:52.910
This is very exciting; talk about driving
transparency, it is an incredible step forward.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:59.320
There is just one word of warning. We are
publishing that formatively for the rest of

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:05.070
this year, so it will be less than perfect
and so on and so forth, but it will be in

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:11.940
the public domain. By 1st&nbsp;April next year,
we hope to have a set of information in each

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:17.990
of those domains which really lets us penetrate
which parts of the country are delivering

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:22.611
this plan, which parts are struggling and
where we need to focus efforts. So we are

00:19:22.611 --> 00:19:29.050
very excited about that, too.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Is there information about

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:31.790
the patient experience outcomes?
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Outcomes will form a part

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:35.880
of what we publish. The other thing I will
say – and I had a meeting on this just this

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:42.330
morning – is this is a suite of information
that will grow. What we will see will be the

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:48.400
beginning, not the end.
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Stephen, to pick up on your word,

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:53.410
‘expertise’; this is obviously a very
specialised area of activity even within psychiatry,

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:59.100
which is a bit of a speciality. When this
rolls out to second wave, are we thinking

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:04.540
about the training of staff to actually fill
that second wave? Is there a danger that we

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:10.130
might end up with a roll-out that we cannot
actually find the expert staff to make it

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:15.730
work?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: Workforce issues are on our

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:21.830
risk register, yes; that is true. What I would
say is, all of the sites we have selected

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:28.180
– and there were quite a number that we
shortlisted but did not select – are already

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:34.080
providing these types of services. I know
it has been badged as new care models, but

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:41.160
in a way it is really about establishing good,
effective local community services that people

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:45.640
are actually already familiar with; they do
know how to do it. Of course, there is the

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:52.690
challenge of getting, as you say, those specialist
people in what is already a sub‑specialty,

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:59.210
who are able to staff and lead those new services.
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Have you flagged that with Health

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:03.520
Education England or with those who are responsible
for training, in the Royal College?

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:06.171
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: Yes, that is something that
we will need to do. We have not done that

00:21:06.171 --> 00:21:12.059
formally thus far. I think we are in the stage
where we are just getting into the detail;

00:21:12.059 --> 00:21:19.330
by December, when we have the full business
cases, it will map out exactly what the new

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:23.640
services will look like, and we will have
a clearer view then of the range of staffing

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:27.460
and the numbers. Thank you, that was a helpful
prompt.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:31.650
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Just to add and just to
really reiterate: across the whole of the

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:37.830
mental health programme, workforce is a considerable
issue. We are used to thinking, particularly

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:43.010
in the current climate, that it is money,
but actually workforce is the major issue

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:48.970
here. We are working very closely with Health
Education England to scope not only the numbers

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:54.830
of staff we need, but capabilities, training
and so on and so forth, and we are also creating

00:21:54.830 --> 00:22:00.429
new roles within that so it is not all just
about more of the same. It is a major undertaking,

00:22:00.429 --> 00:22:08.220
an incredible piece of work, and one of my
jobs working with colleagues is to enable

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:14.210
people who are thinking about careers to understand
that this – mental health, across the range

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:19.049
of programmes – is going to be a very, very
rewarding career. We have to make mental health

00:22:19.049 --> 00:22:23.769
an exciting and rewarding place to work, and
it is, says someone who has worked in it for

00:22:23.769 --> 00:22:28.860
33 years.
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Claire, I had the pleasure

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:35.910
and even excitement of spending a day with
250&nbsp;commissioners and providers and people

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:40.520
involved in this sector, to look at the way
that information and digital technologies

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:47.760
are going to transform both self-care but
also care in the community and care in the

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:56.360
home. This was a relatively unstructured event,
but it was hugely attended, so it tells you

00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:01.400
that there is a huge amount of innovation
going on in this sector, with some extraordinary

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:08.840
results, actually. What are you going to do
in the six pilots that you have, to test out

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:17.480
some of the new ways in which we can completely
transform through digital technology and information?

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:24.640
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: I think one of the obvious
things that springs to mind at the moment,

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:32.360
given that they will need to be assessing
and developing assertive discharge arrangements

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:37.070
for people placed hundreds of miles away,
and often large numbers of them, is to use

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:45.570
technology to facilitate that; so for instance,
telemedicine, so that case reviews, care programme

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:51.070
approach meetings, can take place in a robust
way without having to have, say, a consultant

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:58.910
psychiatrist from the South West, for example,
driving 250 or 300 miles to see the patient.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:03.600
That is one of the first clear ways.
There are also obviously things like digital

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:09.050
devices that people could and should be using,
and the best trusts are, so that when they

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:13.900
go and see people, they have all the information
there with them, they can log in to their

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:18.960
patient information system, and they can complete
their assessments in real time with that person

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:23.100
and then send it back to base. A couple of
examples there.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.320
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I would like to stay in touch
with you around the results of the six wave‑one

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:31.030
pilots, because I think it could be incredibly
interesting in terms of how we replicate that

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:34.080
and scale it quickly.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: Yes.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:41.200
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: If I could just finally pick
up on Victor’s point around targeting and

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:46.160
outcomes and measurement here. Because I think
it is worth noting that today, in fact, a

00:24:46.169 --> 00:24:50.570
very important piece of work by the National
Centre for Social Research was published,

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:55.010
the seven-yearly study of the prevalence of
mental health problems and the extent to which

00:24:55.010 --> 00:25:00.400
the NHS is or is not offering treatment for
them. The good news is that, compared with

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:05.120
when they looked in 2007, the proportion of
people with a common mental disorder getting

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:11.200
treated by the NHS has gone up from one in
four to one in three. The bad news is that

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:17.950
there were very sharp increases in the number
of young women needing services, and when

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:28.289
you standardise for need, it appears that
adults between 35 and 54 are getting a good

00:25:28.289 --> 00:25:37.030
deal, white British origin individuals are
getting a good deal, as in fact are women;

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:43.350
but black potential service users are substantially
undertreated across NHS mental health services.

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:47.670
That just reinforces what we have known for
many years about race and mental health, but

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:51.840
I think as we think about the implementation
of the programme that Claire and Stephen have

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:58.650
set out, we have to see every one of the modules,
the programmes we are putting in place, through

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:03.900
the lens of unmet need and the race issue
in the mental health system as well, and today’s

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:11.970
survey results reinforce that.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Firn: I think it is appropriate

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:16.370
just to do a very brief response to that,
because I welcome that; thank you, Simon.

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:21.919
What we have already done – and this was
part of the selection criteria – is ask

00:26:21.919 --> 00:26:27.950
each of the sites to be absolutely clear about
how they will assess the patient cohort that

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:32.940
they are anticipating to bring back for the
whole range of protected characteristics.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:37.380
Particularly in the adult secure services,
there is a large overrepresentation of people

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:43.130
from BME communities, so they need to be clear
that those local community services that they

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:46.590
are setting up are culturally appropriate,
and that is one of the things we have been

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:50.919
measuring them by.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Via you, Bruce, can I thank

00:26:50.919 --> 00:26:56.191
you both, Claire and Stephen, for what I think
has been a hugely enlightening session. What

00:26:56.191 --> 00:27:01.480
is so important for us as a Board is being
able to track progress in an area where we

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:06.520
have had a task force, we have clear policies
and strategies, to be able to follow it through.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:12.070
Let us do two things. One, if you would pick
up on Noel’s proposal for further conversations

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:18.230
about the digital side; and secondly, if we
can organise a further briefing for the Board

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:23.120
and a development session so that we can get
a full grip not just on progress to date,

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:27.350
but on where this is going, and some of the
bigger issues, including those which Simon

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:32.000
has just shone a light on in his comments,
I think that would be much appreciated. Thank

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:35.780
you very much for coming today and educating
us.

